# AzarRunFlow

Lightweight experiment tracking and reproducibility for research code.

```text
Research project
    ↓
Hydra / optional hydra-zen
    ↓
AzarRunFlow
    ↓
MLflow
```

## Key commands

Build the Docker image:

```bash
make build
```

Run tests:

```bash
make test
```

Run the end-to-end smoke test:

```bash
make smoke
```

Open a Docker shell:

```bash
make shell
```

Start MLflow:

```bash
make mlflow
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost:5001
```

Clean local Docker/MLflow state:

```bash
make clean
```

> `make clean` deletes local MLflow runs/artifacts, so do not use it if you want to keep them.

## Experiment workflow

Experiment YAMLs live in the research project, e.g.

```text
eigenfun/
├── experiments/
│   └── run.py
└── configs/
    ├── config.yaml
    ├── dataset/
    ├── solver/
    └── loss/
```

Run the default Hydra experiment:

```bash
python experiments/run.py
```

Override values:

```bash
python experiments/run.py \
    dataset=fastmri \
    loss.alpha=0.3 \
    seed=7
```

Run a sweep:

```bash
python experiments/run.py -m \
    loss.alpha=0.1,0.2,0.3 \
    seed=1,2,3
```

## What gets logged

AzarRunFlow records:

```text
resolved config
Git commit / branch / dirty state
Git diff when dirty
package/environment snapshot
seed
runtime
metrics
figures / arrays / checkpoints
failure tracebacks
```

For important runs, preferably commit first:

```bash
git status
git add .
git commit -m "Prepare experiment"
```

Then use MLflow to inspect the run:

```text
http://localhost:5001
```

The most important artifact for reproduction is:

```text
config/resolved.yaml
```

Together with the logged Git commit and environment information, this describes what actually ran.